"A use case diagram relates external actors to goals offered by a system within a declared subject boundary.\n\nUse when: Agree which user goals a system supports. Identify external roles and the capabilities they need.\n\nAvoid when: Detailed execution steps, screens, or data structures are required.\n\nVisual grammar: Actors denote external roles, including other systems. Ovals name goal-oriented use cases inside a padded subject boundary. Associations and explicitly labeled include or extend relationships describe participation and reuse.\n\nBuild: Name the subject and keep actors outside its boundary. Identify goals from actor needs rather than UI buttons. Add participation links before considering include or extend. Place related goals together and route actor links through open spaces.\n\nCheck: Use case names express an actor-visible goal. Include and extend arrow directions follow the selected UML semantics. Actors represent roles rather than particular people. The subject title and use case text remain unobscured by lines.\n\nPitfalls: Using the diagram as a flowchart with arrows between successive goals. Adding include or extend relationships merely to reduce visual repetition.\n\nExample: A Traveler can Reserve Trip and Cancel Booking; a Payment Provider participates in Process Payment.\n\nAlternatives: Activity diagram, System context diagram."
